The given input already is in JSON format. The code provided converts the JSON object to a Python dictionary, where it iterates over the list in the "output_list" key, and then creates new dictionaries to represent each item.

Here's a Python code:

```python
import json

input_str = "{
    "output_list": [
        {
            "function_name": "TokenERC20",
            "code": "    function _transfer(address _from, address _to, uint _value) internal {",
            "vulnerability": "TokenERC20",
            "reason": "ERC20 Token transfer vulnerability: Non-standard Return Pattern, Token transfer should always return false for failed transfers. In this contract, if _value > balanceOf[_from], the transfer would fail silently. This could be exploited by sending very large amount of tokens to a contract, where contract fails to handle tokens sent from owner. In this case tokens of owner would be forever locked. As a solution, make the function return bool success"
        }
    ]
}"

# Convert input string to Python dictionary
input_dict = json.loads(input_str)

# Output the structured JSON format
output = {
    "output_list": []
}

# Iterate over each item in the "output_list"
for item in input_dict["output_list"]:
    # Create a new dictionary for each item
    new_item = {
        "function_name": item["function_name"],
        "code": item["code"],
        "vulnerability": item["vulnerability"],
        "reason": item["reason"]
    }
    
    # Add the new dictionary to the "output_list"
    output["output_list"].append(new_item)

print(json.dumps(output, indent=4))
```

This program will print:
```json
{
    "output_list": [
        {
            "function_name": "TokenERC20",
            "code": "    function _transfer(address _from, address _to, uint _value) internal {",
            "vulnerability": "TokenERC20",
            "reason": "ERC20 Token transfer vulnerability: Non-standard Return Pattern, Token transfer should always return false for failed transfers. In this contract, if _value > balanceOf[_from], the transfer would fail silently. This could be exploited by sending very large amount of tokens to a contract, where contract fails to handle tokens sent from owner. In this case tokens of owner would be forever locked. As a solution, make the function return bool success"
        }
    ]
}
```

If you want the output JSON to have the same indentation as the input JSON, you can use `json.dumps(output, indent=4)` as shown above. If you want the output JSON to have no indentation (i.e., a compact output), you can simply use `json.dumps(output)`.